```java
class Rectangle {
    protected int width, height;
    void setWidth(int w) { width = w; }
    void setHeight(int h) { height = h; }
    int area() { return width * height; }
}

class Square extends Rectangle {
    @Override
    void setWidth(int w) { width = w; height = w; } // forces both to match
    @Override
    void setHeight(int h) { width = h; height = h; }
}
```

`Rectangle`'s contract implicitly promises that `setWidth` and `setHeight` are independent — setting one doesn't touch the other. `Square` breaks that promise: setting either one silently changes both, because a square's invariant (equal sides) forces it.

```java
void resize(Rectangle r) {
    r.setWidth(5);
    r.setHeight(10);
    assert r.area() == 50; // holds for any real Rectangle
}

resize(new Square(2)); // assertion fails: area() is 100, not 50
```

This is the exact caller the violation breaks: it only knows about `Rectangle`'s contract, and that contract implies `area()` should be `50` after these two calls. Pass a `Square` in — which the type system allows, since `Square` *is a* `Rectangle` — and the assertion fails, because `setHeight(10)` silently overwrote the width set moments earlier.

```java
interface Shape { int area(); }

class Rectangle implements Shape {
    int width, height;
    Rectangle(int w, int h) { width = w; height = h; }
    public int area() { return width * height; }
}

class Square implements Shape {
    int side;
    Square(int side) { this.side = side; }
    public int area() { return side * side; }
}
```

Neither class promises anything the other can't honor, because neither extends the other — `Square` no longer inherits a `setWidth`/`setHeight` contract it can't independently satisfy. Both simply satisfy `Shape`'s one real, shared promise: producing an `area()`.
